theory.intro:
A negative angle $-\theta$ is a clockwise rotation from the positive $x$-axis. On the unit circle, replacing $\theta$ with $-\theta$ reflects $P(x,y)$ across the $x$-axis to $P'(x,-y)$, establishing the identities $\cos(-\theta)=\cos\theta$, $\sin(-\theta)=-\sin\theta$, and $\tan(-\theta)=-\tan\theta$.

[tikz]
\begin{tikzpicture}[every node/.style={font=\large}, scale=1.8]
\usetikzlibrary{angles, quotes}
\draw[-{Stealth}] (-1.3,0) -- (1.3,0) node[right] {$x$};
\draw[-{Stealth}] (0,-1.3) -- (0,1.3) node[above] {$y$};
\draw (0,0) circle (1);
\coordinate (O) at (0,0);
\coordinate (P) at (0.8,0.6);
\coordinate (Q) at (0.8,-0.6);
\coordinate (A) at (1,0);
\draw[thick] (O) -- (P);
\draw[thick] (O) -- (Q);
\draw[dashed] (P) -- (Q);
\fill (P) circle (1.2pt) node[above right] {$P(x,y)$};
\fill (Q) circle (1.2pt) node[below right] {$P'(x,-y)$};
\pic[draw, "$\theta$", angle radius=0.5cm, angle eccentricity=1.8] {angle = A--O--P};
\pic[draw, "$-\theta$", angle radius=0.7cm, angle eccentricity=1.7] {angle = Q--O--A};
\node[below left] at (O) {$O$};
\end{tikzpicture}
[/tikz]

foundation.question_text:
[tikz]\begin{tikzpicture}[every node/.style={font=\large}, scale=2]
\usetikzlibrary{angles, quotes}
\draw[-{Stealth}] (-1.3,0)--(1.3,0) node[right,font=\scriptsize]{$x$};
\draw[-{Stealth}] (0,-1.3)--(0,1.3) node[above,font=\scriptsize]{$y$};
\draw (0,0) circle (1);
\coordinate (O) at (0,0);
\coordinate (P) at (0.8,0.6);
\coordinate (Q) at (0.8,-0.6);
\coordinate (A) at (1,0);
\draw[thick,blue] (O)--(P);
\draw[thick,blue] (O)--(Q);
\draw[dashed,gray] (P)--(Q);
\fill[red] (P) circle (1.2pt) node[above right,font=\scriptsize]{$P(0.8,0.6)$};
\fill[red] (Q) circle (1.2pt) node[below right,font=\scriptsize]{$P'$};
\pic[draw, "$\theta$", angle radius=0.6cm, angle eccentricity=1.6] {angle = A--O--P};
\pic[draw, "$-\theta$", angle radius=0.9cm, angle eccentricity=1.5] {angle = Q--O--A};
\end{tikzpicture}[/tikz]
The point $P(0.8,0.6)$ on the unit circle corresponds to angle $\theta$.
Find the coordinates of $P'$ corresponding to $-\theta$, and hence state $\cos(-\theta)$ and $\sin(-\theta)$.
